  "text": ".numberOfLines 1\nAt first, I just copy-and-paste the given equation to help us understand it'a little more clearly:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} &&&&A&B&C&D_b\\\\ &+&&B&C&D&A_b\\\\ \\cline{2-6} &&E&F&G&H&I_b.\\\\ \\end{array}$$\n\n.numberOfLines 2\nI can clearly see that this is a sum of two numbers. The first summand is $A.B.C.D$ and the second summand is $B.C.D.A.$, both written in the base $b$ system:\n\n$$A.B.C.D_b = A\\times b^3 + B\\times b^2 + C\\times b + D$$\n\n$$B.C.D.A_b = B\\times b^3 + C\\times b^2 + D\\times b + A$$\n\n.numberOfLines 3\nNow I can add these two numbers. Notice that the $b^3$ terms cancel each other. This is because $A$ and $B$ are equal, and the $b^2$ and $b$ terms also cancel out because $C$ and $D$ are equal:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA.B.C.D_b + B.C.D.A_b &= (A\\times b^3 + B\\times b^2 + C\\times b + D) + (B\\times b^3 + C\\times b^2 + D\\times b + A) \\\\\n&= (b^3 + b^3)\\times A + (b^2 + b^2)\\times C + (b + b)\\times D + (A + B) \\\\\n&= (2\\times b^3)\\times A + (2\\times b^2)\\times C + (2\\times b)\\times D + (A + B).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.numberOfLines 4\nFor the total number of digits, there are 6 digits in the sum: $E$, $F$, $G$, $H$, and $I$. Notice that it has the same number of digits. This means each digit adds up to the same value, i.e., it is a multiple of 9. For example:\n\n$$A\\times b = \\text{sum of digits} = 9\\times e.$$\n\n.numberOfLines 5\nNow, I have this minus the first summand: $A.B.C.D$:\n\n$$9\\times e - (2\\times b^3)\\times A - (2\\times b^2)\\times C - (2\\times b)\\times D - (A + B).$$\n\n.numberOfLines 6\nNotice that I can divide it by $b$ because it is not divisible by 0 and because I can cancel out the similar values like this: $9$. Notice that everywhere the denominator has writing $A\\times b$, the value is like $b$, and we have $9$ left over:\n\n$$\\frac{9e}{b} - 2\\times b^2 - 2\\times b - 1 = 9\\times \\frac{E}{b} - 2\\times b^2 - 2\\times b - 1 = 9\\times e.$$\n\n.numberOfLines 8\nNow we can solve for $b$. Notice that if it is divisible by 9, then $b$ should be an integer value because $e$ is an integer value. Dividing by 9 left over, we can also see that $b$ must be a multiple of 9. We can plug in $b=9,$ $b=18,$ and $b=27,$ and find out which guess value satisfies the equation perfectly:\n\n$$\\frac{e}{b} - e = \\frac{e}{9} - e = \\frac(e  - 81e)}{9} = -\\frac{80e}{9}\\neq 0.$$\n\n.numberOfLines 9\nTherefore, the appropriate value for $b$ should be something less that 27. We can try with $b=15,$ $b=18,$ and $b=21$, and now we have:\n\n$$\\frac{e}{b} - e = \\frac{e}{18} - e = \\frac(e - 162e)}{18} = -\\frac{161e}{18}\\neq 0.$$\n\n.numberOfLines 10\nAgain, we can see that $b=18$ wasn't work. We should try out $b=8,$ $b=10,$ and $b=12$ like this:",
